+/// Compare the relocation ADDEND, where that is a meaningful thing to do.
+///
+/// A relocated operand is rendered (and diffed) as its target symbol, because the
+/// linked address is not yet known. But a format with an implicit addend keeps the
+/// addend in the instruction's displacement, i.e. INSIDE that masked operand, so
+/// `g_tbl + 0x20000` and `g_tbl + 0x1fffe` otherwise score identical even though one
+/// of them indexes a different array element.
+///
+/// Two guards keep this from firing on differences that are not differences: the
+/// architecture decides whether the type's addend is symbol-relative at all
+/// (site-relative ones like `REL32` legitimately differ between two objects), and
+/// the two sides must resolve to the SAME symbol - addends against different symbols
+/// are not commensurable, and naming an interior address after a neighbouring symbol
+/// shifts the addend without moving the referenced byte.
+pub(crate) fn addend_eq(
+    obj: &Object,
+    left: ResolvedRelocation,
+    right: ResolvedRelocation,
+) -> bool {
+    left.relocation.addend == right.relocation.addend
+        || left.symbol.name != right.symbol.name
+        || !obj.arch.reloc_addend_is_symbol_offset(left.relocation.flags)
+}
